# dmarc-monitor — the whole deployment.
#
# Install with `crontab -e` on the server, once. There is nothing else to set up
# and nothing to log in for again: the entry downloads the binary if it is
# missing, and the binary updates itself from published releases from then on.
#
#   crontab -l | grep dmarc-monitor     # what is installed
#   crontab -e                          # paste the block below
#
# Requires curl and a filled-in ~/.local/share/dmarc-monitor/credentials.env.
# That file is the one thing that cannot be bootstrapped — it holds the mailbox
# password — so it has to be put on the server by hand the first time.

# Where cron sends failures. A run that cannot reach the mailbox, or cannot send
# an alert, exits non-zero and mails here — which is the only thing that would
# otherwise go unnoticed, since a monitor that has silently stopped looks
# exactly like a monitor with nothing to report. Leave it empty to disable.
MAILTO=you@example.com

# Runs at 08:00 and 20:00 US Central, on a server in any timezone.
#
# Cron wakes hourly and the first clause throws away the 22 wakeups that are
# not the right hour in Chicago. That looks roundabout, and there is a reason:
#
#   Debian and Ubuntu cron CANNOT schedule in another timezone. Its crontab(5)
#   says so under LIMITATIONS -- "it currently does not support per-user
#   timezones... even if a user specifies the TZ environment variable in his
#   crontab this will affect only the commands executed in the crontab, not the
#   execution of the crontab tasks themselves". A CRON_TZ line looks like it
#   works, is silently ignored for scheduling, and leaves the job firing at
#   German local time -- seven hours out. The hourly guard below is the
#   workaround that same man page recommends.
#
# Asking Chicago what time it is, rather than computing an offset from Berlin,
# is also what keeps this right across daylight saving. The two zones change on
# different dates: for 28 days a year the gap is six hours instead of seven, so
# a crontab with German clock times hardcoded is an hour wrong every March and
# October. This fires exactly twice a day through all four transitions.
#
# Reports arrive once a day, so twice is enough to see one within twelve hours,
# and it is gentle on the mail host; a run takes about a second.
#
# The binary takes a lock, so a run that overruns is never joined by the next
# one, and a hand-run copy cannot collide with a scheduled one.
#
#   min hour        dom mon dow
0 * * * * H="$(TZ=America/Chicago date +\%H)"; [ "$H" = 08 ] || [ "$H" = 20 ] || exit 0; D="$HOME/.local/bin"; L="$HOME/.local/state/dmarc-monitor"; B="$D/dmarc-monitor"; mkdir -p "$D" "$L"; [ -x "$B" ] || { curl -fsSL "https://github.com/__REPO__/releases/latest/download/dmarc-monitor-linux-amd64" -o "$B" && chmod +x "$B"; }; "$B" -cron >> "$L/cron.log" 2>&1 || echo "dmarc-monitor failed; see $L/cron.log"

# Notes on the line above, in the order the traps bite:
#
# * The backslash in `date +\%H` is required. cron translates an unescaped
#   percent sign into a newline, which would truncate the command mid-guard.
#   That is also why the directory is a separate D variable rather than the
#   obvious "${B%/*}", which cannot be escaped its way out of trouble.
#
# * mkdir runs before the redirect's directory is needed, because a redirect
#   into a directory that does not exist fails the whole entry before anything
#   runs.
#
# * HOME is left alone deliberately. The credentials and state files are found
#   relative to it, so a crontab that overrides HOME sends the program looking
#   for its password somewhere it is not.
#
# * The binary is fetched only when absent. Updates are the program's own job,
#   from published releases with verified checksums, so this line never has to
#   change again.
#
# * The trailing `|| echo` is what makes MAILTO work. cron mails whatever a job
#   writes, so a job that redirects everything into a log is a job that mails
#   nothing -- including on the day it fails. Sending the detail to the log and
#   one line to stdout on failure means a quiet mailbox on a normal day and a
#   mail on a bad one.
#
# * cron.log grows by a few lines twice a day. If that ever matters:
#   /etc/logrotate.d, or add `-log json` and ship it somewhere.
#
# On an arm64 server (a Pi, an Ampere VM) change the asset name to
# dmarc-monitor-linux-arm64.

# Check the timezone is actually available before trusting any of this. If
# tzdata is missing, `date` falls back to UTC without complaining and the job
# runs at 08:00 and 20:00 UTC instead -- two hours out in winter, three in
# summer. `dmarc-monitor -check` reports this too.
#
#   TZ=America/Chicago date        # should print Central time, CST or CDT
#
# Useful by hand, on the server:
#
#   ~/.local/bin/dmarc-monitor -version
#   ~/.local/bin/dmarc-monitor -check              # mailbox and relay reachable?
#   ~/.local/bin/dmarc-monitor -once -dry-run      # what would it send right now
#   tail -n 50 ~/.local/state/dmarc-monitor/cron.log
